<onlyinclude>'''Photolithography''' is a coupled optical-imaging, photochemical and process-control operation. Its output is a developed resist geometry with a statistical distribution of edge locations, not merely the aerial image predicted by diffraction theory.</onlyinclude>

==Optical imaging and resolution==
For projection imaging, the empirical Rayleigh-style relationship
:<math>CD=k_1\frac{\lambda}{NA}</math>
organizes first-order scaling. Here <math>k_1</math> is process- and feature-dependent, so a numerical prediction must specify the illumination, mask, resist, and feature class. ASML identifies <math>k_1=0.25</math> as a physical single-exposure limit in its simplified framework; it is not a generally achievable production setting.<ref name="rayleigh">[https://www.asml.com/en/technology/lithography-principles/rayleigh-criterion ASML, ''The Rayleigh criterion for resolution''].</ref>

A commonly used approximate depth-of-focus relation is
:<math>DOF\propto\frac{\lambda}{NA^2}</math>.
At constant wavelength and process factors, changing EUV numerical aperture from 0.33 to 0.55 multiplies nominal resolution <math>CD</math> by <math>0.33/0.55=0.60</math>, while the depth-of-focus scale changes by <math>(0.33/0.55)^2=0.36</math>. Thus the idealized resolution improves by a factor of 1.67 while the depth-of-focus allowance becomes about 36% of its former value. These ratios are not device yield predictions.

'''Illustrative calculation (not a scanner specification):''' for <math>\lambda=13.5\,\mathrm{nm}</math> and an assumed <math>k_1=0.40</math>, <math>CD=16.36\,\mathrm{nm}</math> at <math>NA=0.33</math> and <math>CD=9.82\,\mathrm{nm}</math> at <math>NA=0.55</math>. Feature-specific process windows may differ substantially.

==Exposure physics==
The mask modifies incident illumination; projection optics form an aerial intensity distribution. Photoacid generation and diffusion (in chemically amplified resists), stochastic photon and reaction events, post-exposure bake, and development map that distribution into a resist profile. Therefore image contrast alone does not determine line-edge roughness, defect probability or critical-dimension uniformity.

DUV projection uses refractive optics; immersion raises the accessible numerical aperture. At EUV wavelengths most materials absorb strongly, so multilayer reflective optics operate in vacuum. High-NA EUV raises NA to 0.55 from 0.33, trading improved resolution for a tighter focus budget and additional optical and mask challenges.<ref name="optics">[https://www.asml.com/en/technology/lithography-principles/lenses-and-mirrors ASML, ''Lenses and mirrors''].</ref>

==Pattern placement and process integration==
Registration between successive masks is measured as overlay. Feature-edge placement is affected by overlay, optical proximity effects, mask errors, focus, dose, resist statistics and downstream etch bias. These terms are not necessarily independent; root-sum-square combination requires justified correlation assumptions. Optical proximity correction and illumination optimization can improve a specified process window but cannot eliminate stochastic defects or alignment errors.<ref name="k1">[https://www.asml.com/en/technology/lithography-principles/pushing-k1-further ASML, ''Pushing k1 further''].</ref>

A developed resist pattern is an intermediate artifact. The wafer undergoes etching, implantation, lift-off or other processes before the intended material geometry exists. Multi-patterning adds process steps and overlay constraints; lithography throughput, defectivity and cost must therefore be evaluated at the integrated-flow level.

==Scope and limitations==
The Rayleigh equations describe a useful first-order optical scaling model. They do not uniquely predict two-dimensional corner fidelity, stochastic bridge/break defects, yield, resist collapse, edge-placement error or final etched dimensions. The illustrative calculation assumes identical <math>k_1</math> across optical systems and is not a production-node comparison. Reported feature size should specify measurement convention (e.g. half-pitch versus isolated critical dimension) and metrology conditions.

